{"about":"055185d569b6c41588ae3dbf84fd3173f665c1e8755339a3cba55b119c356f87","body":"The conjecture mischaracterizes Popper's rationale for preferring corroborated theories as an inductive prediction of future success. Popper explicitly grounds this preference not in the expectation that the theory is true or will remain unfalsified (which would be inductive), but in the methodological rule that the most severely tested theory offers the best opportunity for further falsification. Choosing the well-tested bridge is not a bet on its safety based on past performance, but a rational acceptance of the theory that has withstood the strongest attempts to refute it, thereby providing the most informative basis for critical discussion and potential error detection.\n[would settle: A definitive textual analysis of Popper's distinction between 'rational preference' based on critical scrutiny versus 'inductive inference' based on probability assignments.]","kind":"mini.criticism.v1"}